---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MortgagePayoffCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/MortgagePayoffCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="mortgage-payoff-calculator">
  <MortgagePayoffCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the mortgage payoff calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The mortgage payoff calculator builds the loan schedule month by month twice: once with the normal
      payment and once with your extra payments. Each month the interest is the balance times the monthly
      rate, rounded to the cent. The payment pays that interest first; the rest and any extra payment
      reduce the balance.
    </p>
    <p>
      The monthly payment is <code>M = P × i ÷ (1 − (1 + i)^−n)</code>, where <code>P</code> is the
      balance, <code>i</code> is the annual rate divided by 12 and <code>n</code> is the number of months
      left. With a fixed extra payment <code>E</code> each month, the number of months to pay off is
      <code>n = −ln(1 − P × i ÷ (M + E)) ÷ ln(1 + i)</code>.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A balance of 250,000 at 6.5% with 25 years left has a payment of 1,688.02 a month. Pay 200 more each
      month and the loan ends after 19 years 6 months instead of 25 years. Total interest falls from
      256,405 to 191,477, so the extra payments save 64,928.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The rate is fixed for the rest of the loan. Property tax, insurance and escrow are not included.
      Some loans charge a prepayment penalty, and some lenders hold extra money until the next due date
      instead of applying it to the principal at once. Check both with your lender.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
